course: biology
lesson: 157
title: The Amazon forest floor: leafcutters, army ants, tapirs, jaguars and the giant spider
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Under the canopy the forest floor is dim, hot and damp, and so busy that a fallen tree can vanish in a few years as fungi, termites and bacteria eat it.
explain: A line of leafcutter ants marches past holding green leaf pieces like little sails, but they do not eat the leaves: they feed them to a fungus garden underground, and the fungus is their food.
explain: A tapir slips into a stream to escape, a jaguar cracks a turtle shell with one bite, and a Goliath birdeater spider as wide as a dinner plate eats a big insect on a leaf.
explain: In cartoons cats hate water, like Tom shaking his wet fur; the jaguar loves rivers and swims to catch caimans, a kind of crocodile, and fish.
fact: Leafcutter ants cut and carry leaves but do not eat them; they grow a fungus on chewed leaf paste and eat the fungus.
fact: A big leafcutter nest can hold millions of ants, and they have farmed fungus for millions of years, long before people farmed.
fact: Army ants raid in a moving carpet of hundreds of thousands, and antbirds follow to catch the insects fleeing in front, not to eat the ants.
fact: Army ants have no fixed nest and build a living nest of their own linked bodies each night.
fact: The bullet ant has one of the most painful insect stings known, and the pain can last for many hours.
fact: Tapirs are big plant-eaters with a short flexible snout like a little trunk, and they hide in water from jaguars; they are closer relatives of horses and rhinos than of pigs.
fact: The jaguar has the strongest bite for its size of any big cat and can crack turtle shells and caiman skulls.
fact: The Goliath birdeater is the heaviest spider in the world, with a leg span like a dinner plate, and mostly eats insects, worms and frogs, only rarely a bird.
fact: Glass frogs have see-through bellies that show their insides, even the heart beating, and while asleep they hide most of their red blood in their liver to be even harder to see.
fact: On the hot wet forest floor dead wood and leaves rot very fast, and most of the forest's goodness is held in living plants, not the thin soil.
fact: Buttress roots spread out like walls around many rainforest trees and hold the tall tree up in thin soil.
fact: Agoutis open hard Brazil nut pods and bury spare seeds, and the forgotten seeds grow into new Brazil nut trees.
fact: Common vampire bats walk on the ground, make a tiny cut and lap a little blood, and their saliva stops the blood from clotting.
exercise: What do leafcutter ants do with the leaves they carry? | Grow a fungus on them and eat the fungus | Eat the leaves straight away | Build roofs for their nest | Make little umbrellas to keep the rain off the nest
exercise: How long have leafcutter ants been farming? | Millions of years | About a hundred years | Since people taught them | They started last century
exercise: Why do birds follow a swarm of army ants? | To catch the insects fleeing in front of the swarm | To eat the army ants | To ride on the ants | To steal the ants' eggs while the soldiers are busy
exercise: Where do army ants sleep at night? | In a living nest made of their own linked bodies | In a deep hole they dig under the roots of a tree | In empty bird nests | In a fungus garden
exercise: What is the bullet ant famous for? | One of the most painful insect stings | Carrying bullets home | Being the biggest ant in the whole world, as long as a finger | Flying faster than birds
exercise: Why does a tapir dive under the water? | To escape jaguars and other hunters | To catch fish | To wash its snout | To find a mate
exercise: What does a tapir use its short flexible snout for? | Grabbing leaves and fruit, like a little trunk | Digging deep burrows | Sniffing out fish | Squirting water at enemies, the way an elephant does
exercise: Which animals are the tapir's closer relatives? | Horses and rhinos | Pigs and wild boars | Elephants and mice | Cows, goats and sheep
exercise: What is special about the jaguar's bite? | It is the strongest for its size of any big cat | It is poisonous | It is the weakest of all the big cats, so it hunts only small prey | It has no teeth at the front
exercise: What does a Goliath birdeater mostly eat? | Insects, worms and frogs | Mostly birds | Only fruit | Fish from rivers
exercise: How big is the Goliath birdeater's leg span? | About as wide as a dinner plate | About as wide as a small round coin | About as wide as a door, big enough for a child to sit on | About as wide as a small family car
exercise: What can you see through a glass frog's belly? | Its insides, even its heart beating | Nothing, it is black | The leaf behind only | Its eggs only, which it carries inside, and nothing else at all
exercise: Why does a fallen tree disappear in a few years? | Fungi, termites and bacteria break it down fast in the heat and wet | People come and take it | Elephants eat it | It sinks down into the ground whole and stays there for many years before it rots
exercise: Where is most of the Amazon's goodness stored? | In living plants, not in the thin soil | Deep in the rich dark soil under the forest floor | In the thick river mud along the banks only | In the rocks and stones deep under the forest
exercise: Why do many rainforest trees have buttress roots? | The soil is thin, so wide roots hold the tall tree up | To store water for dry years | To keep animals away | To catch falling fruit
exercise: How do agoutis help Brazil nut trees? | They bury seeds and the forgotten ones sprout | They eat all the nuts so the trees have to grow taller to make more | They water the young trees | They climb up and pick the flowers
exercise: Why does a vampire bat's tiny cut keep bleeding? | Its saliva stops the blood from clotting | It bites very deep with its long sharp teeth | It sucks very hard with its strong round mouth | The cow is already ill and its blood is too thin
test: In a cartoon a cat runs screaming from a bath. What is true of the jaguar? | It loves water and swims to hunt caimans and fish | Jaguars never go near water, because their fur would get too heavy | Jaguars cannot swim | All cats sink in water
test: «that ant line's carrying salad for dinner» What is right? | They are carrying leaves to grow fungus, which is what they eat | Yes, ants love leaf salad | They are building a leafy roof | They are taking the leaves down to the river to make little boats
test: A spider has 8 legs. Two Goliath birdeaters sit on a log, and a third one comes. How many legs? | 24 | 16 | 18 | 32
test: A tapir and a jaguar stand by a stream. How many legs and tails altogether? | 8 legs and 2 tails | 6 legs and 2 tails | 8 legs and 1 tail | 4 legs and 2 tails
test: «it's a pig with a nose like an elephant» Which is right about the tapir? | It looks piggy but is a closer cousin of horses and rhinos | It is a baby elephant | It is a wild pig | It is a kind of anteater with a short trunk for licking up ants
test: What would happen if agoutis disappeared from the forest? | Fewer Brazil nut seeds would be planted, and fewer new trees would grow | Brazil nut trees would grow faster with nobody taking their nuts away and burying them | Nothing would happen | More Brazil nuts would fall
test: «a birdeater?? so it eats birds every day lol» What is right? | Despite its name it mostly eats insects, worms and frogs | Yes, a bird for every meal | It eats only parrots | It eats only bird eggs
test: A man stung by a bullet ant says it felt like being shot. What is true? | Its sting is one of the most painful known, and the pain lasts hours | He is joking, it just feels like a tiny mosquito bite that is gone in a minute | Bullet ants shoot real bullets | Bullet ants cannot sting
test: «why don't the trees just grow deep roots like at home» What is the best answer? | The rich layer of soil is thin, so roots spread wide and buttresses hold the tree up | Their roots are too lazy | The trees are too short to need deep roots, so they never grow them and just lean on each other | Deep roots would get eaten by jaguars
test: A glass frog sleeps on a leaf and turns even more see-through. How? | It hides most of its red blood in its liver | It drinks lots of water | It peels off its skin | It turns off its heart
test: Antbirds dart about at the front of an army ant swarm. What are they doing? | Snatching insects that flee the ants | Eating the army ants one by one as they march | Leading the ants like a guide through the forest | Fighting the ants to take over their nest
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the diet of leafcutter ants. | They subsist upon a fungus cultivated upon chewed leaf material | They subsist upon the leaves which they carry | They subsist upon the sap of trees | They subsist upon other insects exclusively
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a fallen tree disappears within a few years upon the floor of the rainforest. | Decomposition by fungi, termites and bacteria proceeds rapidly in heat and moisture | Removal by large mammals for nesting material | Submersion of the material beneath the soil by its own weight over a great many years | Consumption by jaguars
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationships of the tapir? | It is more closely related to the horse and the rhinoceros than to the pig | It is a member of the family of pigs | It is a juvenile form of the elephant | It is a variety of anteater
exam: Ascertain the principal function of the buttress roots of rainforest trees. | They support the tall tree in a thin layer of soil by spreading widely | They store water against prolonged drought | They repel animals that would gnaw the trunk by means of long sharp spines | They capture fruit falling from the canopy
